\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $p_1=2,p_2=3,\ldots$ be the primes in increasing order and $P_j=\prod_{i=1}^j p_i$ their primorials. Let $\mathcal S=\{\prod_{j\in J}P_j:J\subseteq\mathbb N\text{ finite}\}$, with the empty product equal to $1$; each primorial may occur at most once. This is OEIS A129912. Two integers are adjacent when their absolute difference is $1$. The author-confirmed distance restriction is included as in the OEIS comment.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every odd prime $p$, does there exist $s\in\mathcal S$ such that either $|p-s|=1$, or $|p-s|$ is a prime $q<p$? \href{https://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/maceachen_conjecture}{[S2]} \href{https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oeis/oeisdata/main/seq/A129/A129912.seq}{[S3]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Is every odd prime within distance one, or within a smaller prime distance, of a product of distinct primorials?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 3345 (OPG-671). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Open Problem Garden, MacEachen Conjecture, node 671, original problem page. Original question and full discussion. \url{https://www.openproblemgarden.org/op/maceachen_conjecture}.
\item[S3] Bill McEachen, OEIS A129912, Numbers that are products of distinct primorial numbers, author comment of 3 June 2010 (clarified 26 February 2019), official OEIS data repository. \url{https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oeis/oeisdata/main/seq/A129/A129912.seq}.
\end{description}
